## What this blueprint produces

Running `forge new {{ .Name }} my-notes-app` creates:

- `index.html` — note input form + localStorage-backed list
- `style.css` — minimalist black/gray/white styling
- `app.js` — note persistence via localStorage
- `.understand-anything/knowledge-graph.json` — project knowledge graph

## Testing locally

Push this directory to a git remote, then install and scaffold:

```bash
git push origin main
forge install <your-git-url>
forge new {{ .Name }} my-notes-app --yes --no-hooks
open my-notes-app/index.html
```

## Adding a page

From inside a scaffolded project:

```bash
forge add page --var page_name=about
```

Creates `page.html` linked back to the main page.

## Extending this blueprint

### Add a template variable

In `manifest.yaml`, add to `variables:`:

```yaml
  - name: my_var
    prompt: "What value?"
    type: string
    default: "default"
```

Rename any file in `template/` from `file.ext` to `file.ext.tmpl` and reference
the variable in the content as `{{ "{{" }}.MyVar{{ "}}" }}` (PascalCase key).

### Add static files

Drop any file under `template/`. Files without a `.tmpl` suffix are copied verbatim —
no variable expansion, safe for binary files or files that contain their own
template syntax.

### Add an extension

1. Create `extensions/<name>/template/` with the files to add.
2. Declare it in `manifest.yaml` under `extensions:`.
3. Optionally create `extensions/<name>/graph-fragment.yaml` to update the
   knowledge graph when the extension is applied.

### Publish

Anyone can install your blueprint with:

```bash
forge install <git-url>
```
